questions.answer:
const safeJsonParse = (raw, fallback = null) => {
  try {
    return JSON.parse(raw);
  } catch {
    return fallback;
  }
};

console.log(safeJsonParse('{"id":1}'));            // { id: 1 }
console.log(safeJsonParse("{bad", { error: true })); // { error: true }

Optional catch binding (catch {}) is ES2019 and is appropriate when the error value is not needed. Return the fallback explicitly so the function has one predictable return type. If you need to report the failure, log it or return a result object such as { ok: false, error } instead of silently swallowing it — the point is that parsing bad input is expected, not that failures should be invisible.

