questions.answer:
const retry = async (fn, attempts = 3) => {
  let lastError;
  for (let i = 1; i <= attempts; i++) {
    try {
      return await fn();
    } catch (err) {
      lastError = err;
    }
  }
  throw lastError;
};

let calls = 0;
const flaky = async () => {
  calls++;
  if (calls < 3) throw new Error(`fail ${calls}`);
  return "success";
};

retry(flaky).then(console.log); // success

await inside try is essential: without it, the rejection escapes and the catch never runs. The loop returns immediately on success; after the final failure, throw lastError preserves the original error and stack. In production you would add a delay between attempts and only retry transient errors (network, 5xx), not validation failures. attempts should be validated as a positive integer to avoid returning undefined.

